format 1
adapter statements 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

statement radiation-and-the-body-s01
verdict: myth
statement: Any dose of radiation is deadly.

explanation:
    Everyone gets small doses every day. It takes a very large dose in a short time, about 1000 millisieverts, to make people ill.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s02
verdict: fact
statement: The average person in the United States gets about 6 millisieverts of radiation a year, and most of it is natural.

explanation:
    The average is about 6.2 millisieverts a year, and most of it comes from natural background radiation.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s03
verdict: fact
statement: Cells in your body are extremely efficient at repairing radiation damage.

explanation:
    Ionizing radiation can damage the DNA in cells, and cells repair most of that damage.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s04
verdict: myth
statement: Radiation is harmless at every dose.

explanation:
    A very large dose in a short time makes people ill, and low doses can add a small chance of cancer over a lifetime.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s05
verdict: fact
statement: A very large dose of radiation in a short time can cause nausea and vomiting within hours.

explanation:
    About 1000 millisieverts in a short time is the threshold for acute radiation syndrome.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s06
verdict: myth
statement: A small dose every day is the same as one large dose.

explanation:
    Dose rate matters. A low dose, or one spread over a long time, carries less risk because repair is more likely.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s07
verdict: fact
statement: Spending less time near a source lowers the dose from it.

explanation:
    Limiting the time you are exposed reduces the dose from the radiation source.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s08
verdict: fact
statement: The dose from a source decreases dramatically as you move away from it.

explanation:
    Increasing your distance from a source is one of the three ways to lower a dose.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s09
verdict: fact
statement: Barriers of lead, concrete or water protect against penetrating gamma rays.

explanation:
    Shielding puts material between you and the source, and it can greatly reduce the dose.

statement radiation-and-the-body-s10
verdict: myth
statement: A person who was exposed to radiation is radioactive.

explanation:
    Exposure is not contamination. Radiation does not get on or in people.
